Phase 1 — Core Java
Topic 1: SOLID Principles
SOLID is a set of five software design principles that help developers write code that is easier to maintain, extend, test and modify.

Why do companies care?

Imagine you're working on an Amazon-like e-commerce application. Initially, it supports credit card payments. Later, the company wants UPI, PayPal and other payment methods.

If your code is poorly designed, adding a new payment method might require modifying several existing classes.

SOLID helps prevent that.
The five principles
S
Single Responsibility Principle

One class = one responsibility



O
Open/Closed Principle

Open for extension, closed for modification



L
Liskov Substitution Principle

Child classes should work wherever their parent is expected



I
Interface Segregation Principle

Don't force classes to implement methods they don't need



D
Dependency Inversion Principle

Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations



1. S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
Definition: A class should have only one reason to change.

Imagine an application that manages customer orders.

A badly designed class:
class Order {

    public void createOrder() {
        System.out.println("Order created");
    }

    public void saveToDatabase() {
        System.out.println("Saved to database");
    }

    public void sendEmail() {
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }

    public void generateInvoice() {
        System.out.println("Invoice generated");
    }
}


What's wrong?

This class has four responsibilities:
- Order creation
- Database operations
- Email notifications
- Invoice generation
If email functionality changes, we must modify the same class that handles orders.

Better design:
class OrderService {
    public void createOrder() {
        System.out.println("Order created");
    }
}

class OrderRepository {
    public void saveToDatabase() {
        System.out.println("Saved to database");
    }
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail() {
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }
}

class InvoiceService {
    public void generateInvoice() {
        System.out.println("Invoice generated");
    }
}


Now each class has one main responsibility.

Real-world Spring Boot connection:
Class	Responsibility
OrderController	Handle HTTP requests
OrderService	Business logic
OrderRepository	Database operations
EmailService	Send emails

Interview question: What is the benefit of SRP?

Answer: It improves maintainability, testability and reduces the impact of changes.

2. O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Definition: Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.

Imagine an application supporting different payment methods.

Bad approach:
class PaymentService {

    public void pay(String type) {

        if (type.equals("UPI")) {
            System.out.println("Pay using UPI");
        }
        else if (type.equals("CARD")) {
            System.out.println("Pay using Card");
        }
    }
}


What happens when we introduce PayPal?

We must modify the existing class.

Better approach:
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}


class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Pay using UPI");
    }
}


class CardPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Pay using Card");
    }
}


class PaypalPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Pay using PayPal");
    }
}


Now each new payment method can be introduced as a new implementation.

Real-world example: Adding a new payment gateway without rewriting your existing payment service.

Interview question: How does OCP help in Spring Boot?

Answer: We can introduce new service implementations through interfaces and dependency injection without continuously modifying existing business logic.